00:14:31.633 --> 00:14:34.050
The next product that I wanted
to dive a little bit deeper

00:14:34.050 --> 00:14:35.830
into was phishing protection.

00:14:35.830 --> 00:14:37.735
And just to outline this--

00:14:37.735 --> 00:14:39.360
the way I like to
think about phishing,

00:14:39.360 --> 00:14:41.735
it's something our team has
worked for more than a decade

00:14:41.735 --> 00:14:45.840
on, is it doesn't matter what
kind of big huge, scary castle

00:14:45.840 --> 00:14:47.540
you build, you
leave the gate open,

00:14:47.540 --> 00:14:48.720
everyone's going to come in.

00:14:48.720 --> 00:14:50.520
And that really is the
case with phishing.

00:14:50.520 --> 00:14:52.180
We build some pretty
amazing defenses.

00:14:52.180 --> 00:14:54.330
We see everyone across industry
build amazing defenses.

00:14:54.330 --> 00:14:55.470
I think everyone in
the room probably

00:14:55.470 --> 00:14:57.940
knows that phishing is still
your number one problem.

00:14:57.940 --> 00:15:01.470
It's people leaving
the door unlocked.

00:15:01.470 --> 00:15:03.780
Really the goal with
phishing protection

00:15:03.780 --> 00:15:06.830
is to stop people from
clicking on phishing, period.

00:15:06.830 --> 00:15:09.042
Right we know that
with enough tries,

00:15:09.042 --> 00:15:10.500
and essentially
the tries are free,

00:15:10.500 --> 00:15:13.600
people will always be fooled
into clicking at some level.

00:15:13.600 --> 00:15:17.670
So we really want to ensure
that as few people see

00:15:17.670 --> 00:15:20.010
those links as possible, and
especially for those links

00:15:20.010 --> 00:15:21.462
that are targeting your brands.

00:15:21.462 --> 00:15:22.920
So, for example,
some of them might

00:15:22.920 --> 00:15:25.710
use say, the Google
logo, or the Gmail logo,

00:15:25.710 --> 00:15:27.590
or things that are
used in our iconography

00:15:27.590 --> 00:15:31.120
for pages to denote their log in
pages offerings to fool users,

00:15:31.120 --> 00:15:33.270
especially using,
say, mobile devices.

00:15:33.270 --> 00:15:35.520
And phishing protection
leverages the power

00:15:35.520 --> 00:15:39.600
of Safe Browsing to not
only classify those pages

00:15:39.600 --> 00:15:41.670
but also to add them to
the Safe Browsing list

00:15:41.670 --> 00:15:43.590
when they match our
policy, in order

00:15:43.590 --> 00:15:46.170
to ensure that users will
see a big red warning that

00:15:46.170 --> 00:15:47.832
looks a little bit
like this color

00:15:47.832 --> 00:15:50.040
before they ever go to a
phishing and a malware page.

00:15:50.040 --> 00:15:51.420
So they're very aware.

00:15:51.420 --> 00:15:52.940
And typically they
don't go there.

00:15:52.940 --> 00:15:55.180
I think our click-through
rate is well below 3%.

00:15:55.180 --> 00:15:56.430
So in the few places
in our business

00:15:56.430 --> 00:15:57.888
where we focus on
having the lowest

00:15:57.888 --> 00:16:00.320
click-through rate possible.

00:16:00.320 --> 00:16:03.780
Just to walk through a
little bit of how this works.

00:16:03.780 --> 00:16:06.810
You as a business can submit
URLs to phishing production.

00:16:06.810 --> 00:16:09.750
So, oftentimes, there's existing
detections from referral links,

00:16:09.750 --> 00:16:11.550
from your own email
and security tools,

00:16:11.550 --> 00:16:13.667
or just from your own
scraping of the web.

00:16:13.667 --> 00:16:15.500
Usually there's some
really smart IT person,

00:16:15.500 --> 00:16:17.875
someone who's written a cool
bot to find things that look

00:16:17.875 --> 00:16:19.225
like your brand on the web.

00:16:19.225 --> 00:16:21.350
You can submit those to
Safe Browsing for crawling,

00:16:21.350 --> 00:16:24.400
so we can confirm that they
are truly indeed malicious.

00:16:24.400 --> 00:16:26.040
We have a team of
about 60 engineers

00:16:26.040 --> 00:16:28.100
who spent the better
part of a decade building

00:16:28.100 --> 00:16:29.850
machine learning to
recognize these things

00:16:29.850 --> 00:16:31.267
and recognize them
as they evolve.

00:16:31.267 --> 00:16:33.660
So we have incredibly good
detections around those.

00:16:33.660 --> 00:16:35.100
We also ingest
data from a variety

00:16:35.100 --> 00:16:38.100
of third party sources or
even first party sources

00:16:38.100 --> 00:16:39.810
from within Google.

00:16:39.810 --> 00:16:42.247
Once those are all
added to Safe Browsing,

00:16:42.247 --> 00:16:43.830
we'll notify you as
a customer, so you

00:16:43.830 --> 00:16:45.455
can institute your
own countermeasures,

00:16:45.455 --> 00:16:48.247
be it take downs, blocks,
notifications, user education,

00:16:48.247 --> 00:16:50.580
whatever it is you've found
work well for your customers

00:16:50.580 --> 00:16:51.960
and your business.

00:16:51.960 --> 00:16:54.360
You'll get that protection on
three billion plus devices.

00:16:54.360 --> 00:16:56.750
I'll note, this is far
beyond the reach of Chrome

00:16:56.750 --> 00:16:59.400
or any Google products.

00:16:59.400 --> 00:17:02.985
The vast majority of
browsers, security companies,

00:17:02.985 --> 00:17:05.651
web-filtering companies
use this list in order

00:17:05.651 --> 00:17:06.609
to protect their users.

00:17:06.609 --> 00:17:08.567
So you get a wide variety
of protections almost

00:17:08.567 --> 00:17:09.900
wherever your users are.

00:17:09.900 --> 00:17:12.150
And then finally, this will
be integrated into the GCP

00:17:12.150 --> 00:17:15.792
reporting analytics through
Cloud Security Command Center.

